<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Warrior River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black Warrior River is a river of west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, flowing past Tuscaloosa to join the Tombigbee. Its name translates that of the Muscogean chief Tuskaloosa. Its fertile valley supported the Mississippian chiefdom centred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moundville-archaeological-site">Moundville</a>, one of the largest mound sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, between about 1120 and 1450.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-warrior-river.jpg" alt="Black Warrior River" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tim Kiser (w:Malepheasant) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Warrior_River.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
